{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A decentralized network is more resilient to correlated failure because it shifts the failure mode from 'systemic collapse' to 'partial degradation' by eliminating the unified control plane as a single point of failure.","counterconditions":["The decentralized services share a critical common dependency (e.g., a single DNS provider or cloud region).","The cost of managing diversity exceeds the benefit, leading to poor maintenance and higher individual failure rates."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Operational diversity (different OS, languages, and deployment pipelines) prevents a single 'poison pill' update or zero-day exploit from propagating across the entire network, whereas a large service's internal redundancy is often undermined by a shared orchestration layer that distributes the same failure to all redundant nodes.","optional_refs":["882bdbe177a5672f29fb46151da9db8c904bc95410e52f9965065baf39f927c7","4397aa6cccf1c9ae985248d41e1218b756ee03787e249ded8fc685908ba43ce2"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A large service with high internal redundancy is more resilient because it can implement 'active-active' dynamic traffic evacuation that decentralized networks cannot coordinate.","counterconditions":["The failure originates within the unified control plane itself (the 'Complexity Paradox').","The traffic volume exceeds the capacity of the remaining healthy segments during evacuation."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"A unified control plane allows for the instantaneous rerouting of 100% of traffic away from a failing segment based on real-time telemetry, whereas decentralized services lack the global state necessary to preemptively avoid correlated failures in neighboring nodes.","optional_refs":["b786fddb3d54b2be5db5cc7b11ffaa911f83a4690f0bba502facdd15b9f423a6","952e7052242f7add5353ab23c9ca1200c6b7d2454f6a4b7b58d0c08335d0d88a"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The 'Recovery Signature' (temporal alignment of restoration) is the primary evidence distinguishing truly independent failures from those with a hidden shared cause.","counterconditions":["The recovery process is triggered by a global automated health-check system that restarts all services simultaneously.","The services are logically independent but physically co-located on the same hardware/hypervisor."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Independent failures occur and resolve according to the local state of individual services; however, if a cluster of seemingly unrelated services all recover within a narrow, synchronized time window, it indicates a shared external trigger (e.g., a BGP route leak or global API outage) that was the actual source of the correlation.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Neither architecture is inherently more resilient; resilience is a function of the ratio between redundancy and shared-fate dependencies.","counterconditions":["One architecture can be proven to have a lower mathematical floor for shared-fate dependencies regardless of scale.","Operational diversity can be implemented within a large service without increasing orchestration complexity."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Adding redundancy (more nodes) in a large service increases the complexity of the orchestration layer, creating new shared-fate dependencies. Similarly, decentralization reduces control but may introduce dependencies on shared public infrastructure. The 'breaking point' occurs when the complexity of managing the resilience mechanism becomes the most likely cause of failure.","optional_refs":["bf1d23583174cb8aff53c8c0590e4e8e48d3c9c0907c6b2321f113f1d8b85126"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.5},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Decentralized networks are less resilient because they suffer from 'death by a thousand cuts'—a high frequency of small, uncorrelated failures that aggregate into a perceived systemic instability.","counterconditions":["The 'noise' failures are isolated and do not impact the overall SLA of the network.","Standardized monitoring tools can be deployed across decentralized services without a unified control plane."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"While a large service avoids frequent small outages through strict standardization, a decentralized network's lack of unified standards leads to a high baseline of 'noise' failures. This makes it harder to detect the onset of a truly correlated systemic failure until it is too late to mitigate.","optional_refs":["cdc1ea24d057819ee90bc9c5943b3229ede77c37dbfcf2c669d7ce01cd57c599"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3}],"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"Hypothesizing the 'Diversity-Efficiency Trade-off': There is a point where the overhead of maintaining diverse implementations (different languages/OS) in a decentralized network creates a 'security debt' (e.g., unpatched old versions) that actually increases the probability of a correlated failure via a common old vulnerability, contradicting the claim that diversity always helps.","why_keep_this":"Challenges the assumption that diversity is a pure win for resilience."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Can we distinguish between 'operational diversity' (different people doing things) and 'technical diversity' (different code doing things) in terms of their impact on correlated failure?","related_refs":[]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}